---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';

const title = 'How to Set Up 2FA on PayPal (TOTP Authenticator)';

const description =
  'Set up PayPal 2-step verification with an authenticator app, then generate live 6-digit TOTP codes in your browser — no phone app needed.';

---

<GuideLayout
  serviceName="PayPal"
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/guides/paypal/"
  dateModified="2026-11-23"
  datePublished="2026-11-10"
  lastReviewed="2026-11-23"
  reviewedPrefix="Fact-checked against"
  reviewedClaim="PayPal’s official help articles (read via a text reader after a 403)"
  sources={[
    {
      label: 'PayPal Help — What is 2-step verification?',
      href: 'https://www.paypal.com/us/cshelp/article/what-is-2-step-verification-help167',
      kind: 'official',
    },
    {
      label: 'PayPal Help — What is multi-factor authentication and a remembered device?',
      href: 'https://www.paypal.com/us/cshelp/article/what-is-multi-factor-authentication-and-a-remembered-device-help1156',
      kind: 'official',
    },
    {
      label: 'PayPal Help — What can I do if I’ve changed my mobile number and can’t log in?',
      href: 'https://www.paypal.com/us/cshelp/article/what-can-i-do-if-ive-changed-my-mobile-number-and-cant-log-in-help678',
      kind: 'official',
    },
    {
      label: 'RFC 6238 — TOTP: Time-Based One-Time Password Algorithm',
      href: 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6238',
      kind: 'independent',
    },
  ]}
  intro="PayPal’s 2-step verification has an authenticator-app option, and that is the method worth choosing: a phone number can be moved to another SIM, and money moves through this account. PayPal names Google Authenticator and Microsoft Authenticator, and documents a five-step setup."
  minutes={4}
  params={['6 digits', '30s period', 'SHA-1']}
  ctaTitle="Your PayPal account is a payment rail — treat the backup like one"
  ctaBody="PayPal documents no recovery-code list, so the setup secret is your way back to codes if the phone goes. Keep the number on the account current too: PayPal’s fallback one-time passcodes still go there, and an old number is how a locked-out owner stays locked out."
  deepDive={{
    heading: 'What PayPal documents, and what it leaves to the screen',
    blocks: [
      {
        title: 'The five steps are documented; the secret screen is not',
        body: 'PayPal’s article is precise about the path — the Settings icon next to “Log out”, then Security, then “Set Up” to the right of “2-step verification”, then “Use an authenticator app” and “Set it Up”. It does not describe what comes next, so the QR-or-key choice is left to your account’s screen. That is why this guide describes both and treats neither label as quoted. One note on access: paypal.com refused this browser with HTTP 403 on 23 November 2026, so the article was read through a text reader; the wording above is from the live page, not a cache.',
      },
      {
        title: 'The phone number remains a factor after setup',
        body: 'PayPal’s multi-factor article says one-time passcodes can arrive by SMS, WhatsApp, email, phone call or certain external apps. Its article on changing your number is blunt: update the number before you switch, or the access code still goes to the old one and you cannot log in. 2-step verification can also be deactivated at any time — so the number is not a legacy detail, it is part of the login.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Remembered devices count as a factor',
        body: 'PayPal remembers a browser or phone after it has confirmed your identity, and that device becomes one of the two factors for later logins. You can review and remove them under Settings → Security → “Manage your logins” → Manage. Removing a device does not sign it out, but PayPal stops using it as a factor — worth knowing on a shared or borrowed machine.',
      },
    ],
  }}
  steps={[
    {
      title: 'Open Settings, then Security',
      body: 'Sign in at paypal.com and click the Settings icon next to “Log out”. Then click “Security” near the top of the page.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Start 2-step verification setup',
      body: 'Click “Set Up” to the right of “2-step verification”. PayPal then asks how you want to receive codes.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Choose “Use an authenticator app”',
      body: 'Select that option and click “Set it Up”. This is the TOTP route — independent of SMS — and the one PayPal names authenticator apps for.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Take the secret from the setup screen',
      body: 'PayPal’s article stops at “follow the steps on the screen”. If a QR code appears, scan or screenshot it; if a Base32 key is printed or linked for manual entry, copy that instead. The generator decodes a screenshot on your device, never on a server.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Generate, verify, activate',
      body: 'Paste the key or screenshot into 2facounter.com, copy the live 6-digit code, and submit it where PayPal asks. 2-step verification flips to active and PayPal starts asking for a code at sign-in.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Keep the fallback honest',
      body: 'Check the number and email on the account — PayPal’s one-time passcodes can arrive by SMS, WhatsApp, email or phone call. Consider a passkey as well; PayPal recommends one as a faster, safer login. Store the setup secret away from the phone so a lost device does not become a lost account.',
    },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'Does PayPal require 2FA?',
      a: 'No — PayPal says it “highly recommends” adding authentication factors, and its article on changing your number notes that 2-step verification can be deactivated at any time. That makes it optional and worth doing: a payment account guarded by a password alone is one credential leak away from a transfer.',
    },
    {
      q: 'SMS codes vs authenticator app on PayPal — which should I pick?',
      a: 'The authenticator app, when it is offered. SMS codes die with your SIM: a SIM-swap attacker who ports your phone number receives your codes. TOTP lives on the device (or in this browser) and never transmits anything. Note that PayPal still uses SMS, WhatsApp, email or a phone call for other one-time passcodes, so the number stays part of the account.',
    },
    {
      q: 'I changed my phone number — what breaks?',
      a: 'PayPal’s documented answer is that the access code keeps going to the old number until you update it in the account, and then you cannot log in. Update the number first, before the old SIM goes dead; if you are already locked out, use PayPal’s account-recovery route and update the number as soon as you are back in.',
    },
    {
      q: 'I lost my phone and can’t get PayPal codes — what now?',
      a: 'If you saved the setup secret, paste it into the generator on another device and keep signing in. PayPal documents no recovery-code list for 2-step verification, so without the secret you are in account recovery, and the number on file is what the fallback passcodes use. A passkey registered in advance is the other documented way in.',
    },
    {
      q: 'PayPal rejects my code right after setup — why?',
      a: 'Most rejections are clock drift: the server and your device disagree on what "now" is, and the code rolls over mid-verification. Sync your system clock automatically, then submit the freshly displayed code. If a code mix-up ever locks you out, recovery exists — but a synced clock prevents the problem in the first place.',
    },
  ]}
/>
